{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Calibri;}{\f1\fnil Calibri;}}
{\*\generator Riched20 10.0.19041}\viewkind4\uc1 
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\f0\fs22\lang9 TASK:1\b0\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b Implementation of Graph search algorithms (Breadth first search and Depth First Search) \par
using following constraints.\par
\ul Aim: \ulnone\b0 To Implement of Graph search algorithms (Breadth first search and Depth \par
First Search) using Python.\par
\ul\b Task 1A\ulnone\b0\par
\ul\b Algorithm:\ulnone\b0\par
BFS\par
Step 1: Start by putting any one of the graph\rquote s vertices at the back of the queue.\par
Step 2: Now take the front item of the queue and add it to the visited list.\par
Step 3: Create a list of that vertex's adjacent nodes. Add those which are not within the \par
visited list to the rear of the queue.\par
Step 4: Keep continuing steps two and three till the queue is empty.\par
Program\par
from collections import deque \par
def bfs(graph, start):\par
queue, visited = deque([start]), set() \par
print("BFS:", end=" ")\par
while queue:\par
node = queue.popleft() \par
if node not in visited:\par
print(node, end=" ") \par
visited.add(node)\par
queue.extend(neighbor for neighbor in graph[node] if neighbor not in visited) \par
print()\par
\b # Example graph:\par
 Graph = \{\par
 1: [2, 3],\par
 2: [4, 5],\par
 3: [6],\par
 4: [],\par
 5: [],\par
 6: []\par
\}\par
bfs(graph, 1)\par
Output:\par
BFS: 1 2 3 4 5 6\par

